for stem,ans in [ ('the principle of superposition.','When two or more waves of the same type meet at a point, the resultant displacement is the algebraic sum of their individual displacements at that point.'),('what is added when waves superpose.','Their instantaneous displacements at the same point are added algebraically.'),('the resultant displacement when two pulses give +3 mm and -2 mm at one point.','The resultant displacement is +1 mm because +3 mm + (-2 mm) = +1 mm.'),('why waves need not have equal amplitudes to superpose.','Superposition applies whenever waves of the same type overlap; equal amplitude is not required.'),('the distinction between amplitude and displacement in superposition.','Amplitude is the maximum displacement, while the signed instantaneous displacements are summed.'),('the resultant when equal and opposite displacements meet.','The displacements cancel, giving zero resultant displacement at that instant.')]:short(1,'Explain',stem,ans,4 if 'pulses' in stem else 2)
for cmd,stem,ans in [('Describe','how a stationary microwave pattern is demonstrated with a transmitter, reflector and detector.','Reflect microwaves back towards the transmitter and move a detector along the path; successive intensity minima or maxima reveal the stationary pattern.'),('Explain','why moving a microwave detector along the beam gives alternating maxima and minima.','The incident and reflected microwaves superpose, giving antinodes and nodes at fixed positions.'),('Describe','how a stretched string can show stationary waves.','Drive a taut string at a suitable frequency; the reflected wave superposes with the incident wave and visible loops form between fixed nodes.'),('Describe','how an air column can demonstrate stationary sound waves.','Drive a tube with sound and vary frequency or air-column length until resonance; displacement nodes and antinodes form at fixed positions.'),('Identify','the displacement conditions at the closed and open ends of an air column.','The closed end is a displacement node and the open end is a displacement antinode, neglecting end corrections.'),('Explain','why powder in a sound tube collects in heaps at particular places.','Air vibrates least at displacement nodes, so powder settles there while stronger motion sweeps it from antinodes.')]:short(2,cmd,stem,ans,4 if 'microwave pattern' in stem else 2)
for cmd,stem,ans in [('Explain','how two progressive waves form a stationary wave.','Two waves of the same type and frequency with similar amplitudes travel in opposite directions and superpose, producing fixed nodes and antinodes.'),('Define','a node in a stationary wave.','A node is a position of zero amplitude; its displacement is zero at all times.'),('Define','an antinode in a stationary wave.','An antinode is a position of maximum amplitude.'),('Explain','why adjacent loops on a stationary string move in antiphase.','The standing-wave displacement changes sign across a node, so adjacent loops have phase difference 180°.'),('Describe','a graphical method for locating nodes.','Draw the two oppositely travelling wave profiles and add their displacements point by point at successive times; fixed positions that always sum to zero are nodes.'),('Compare','particle phases within one loop and in neighbouring loops.','Particles between the same adjacent nodes oscillate in phase; particles in adjacent loops oscillate in antiphase.')]:short(3,cmd,stem,ans,4 if 'graphical' in stem else 2)
for cmd,stem,ans in [('State','the separation between adjacent nodes in terms of wavelength.','Adjacent nodes are separated by half a wavelength, $\\lambda/2$.'),('State','the separation between adjacent antinodes in terms of wavelength.','Adjacent antinodes are separated by $\\lambda/2$.'),('State','the separation between a node and its nearest antinode.','The separation is $\\lambda/4$.'),('Explain','how several node positions give a more reliable wavelength.','Measure across several adjacent node gaps, divide by the number of gaps to find the mean separation, then double it.'),('Calculate','the wavelength when adjacent nodes are 0.30 m apart.','Wavelength is $2(0.30)=0.60$ m.'),('Determine','the wavelength if the first and fifth nodes are 0.80 m apart.','Four node intervals span 0.80 m, so $\\lambda=2(0.80/4)=0.40$ m.')]:short(4,cmd,stem,ans,4 if 'fifth' in stem else 2)
